File previews via tabbed XEmbed with persistent application state.  For text
MIME types, uview uses Neovim's server interface; for media files, mpv's IPC
socket; and for PDFs, Zathura over D-Bus.

uview is intended for FIFO-based file-manager preview workflows (such as nnn)
in an X11 session.  See [.AGENTS/README.md](.AGENTS/README.md) for the
repository's maintainer and agent workflow, including the current tabbed
selection issue and improvement backlog.

Use `uview --zen-mode [FIFO [FILE]]` to start Bookokrat document previews in
zen mode. The default leaves Bookokrat's sidebar visible.

Set `doc_prv=tdf` in `~/.config/uview/config` to use TDF for PDF previews.
Bookokrat remains the default (`doc_prv=bookokrat`) and continues to handle
EPUB and DjVu previews when TDF is selected.

## Configuration

`uview` sources `~/.config/uview/config` when it starts. All settings are
optional; these are the supported overrides:

```sh
# Document previewer: bookokrat (default) or tdf (PDF only)
doc_prv=bookokrat

# Bookokrat: set to y, or use uview --zen-mode
bok_zen=n

# PDF fallback: image (default, rendered through mpv), or another value to
# prefer Zathura when doc_prv is not bookokrat or tdf.
pdf_prv=image
pdf_pag=1
pdf_dpi=100

# Command overrides
mpv=mpv
bookokrat=bookokrat
tdf=tdf

# Poll interval, in seconds, for embedded document-reader changes
tim_lim=0.05

# Set to n only to suppress MIME-driven preview loading while diagnosing.
tim_swi=y
```

`pdf_pag` and `pdf_dpi` apply only to the `pdf_prv=image` fallback. Command
overrides are useful for pinned local builds or wrapper scripts.

The default configuration is installed as
`/usr/local/share/uview/config` (adjusted by `PREFIX`). Install a personal
copy without replacing an existing one with:

```sh
uview --install-config
```

Use `uview --install-config --force` only when you intend to replace the
existing personal configuration.

## Installation

`make install` installs the program to `/usr/local/bin` and the default
configuration to `/usr/local/share/uview/config`. If another `uview` command
is found on `PATH`, it reports the path and asks whether to continue. It never
removes a system-managed package; use the system package manager yourself if
you decide to remove that version.
